Lab Report : 1

Write a program to simulate a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA)

that accepts strings over the alphabet {‘a’ , ‘b’} that

1. End with “ab”

2. Contain an even number of ‘a’s



Introduction : 

This C++ program includes two checks on a user-input string.

The first part verifies whether the string ends with "ab".

The second part counts how many 'a' characters appear in the string.

It checks if the count of 'a's is even using a modulo operation.

If both conditions are true, it confirms the string is valid.



Tools : 

The C++ programs use several standard tools and concepts from the C++ language. The #include <iostream> header is used for input and output operations via cin and cout, while #include <string> enables the use of the string data type and its related functions. The using namespace std; directive is included to simplify syntax by avoiding the repeated use of the std:: prefix. A range-based for loop is used to iterate through each character in the input string, allowing the program to count occurrences of the character 'a'. Conditional statements (if) are applied to check whether the string ends with "ab" and whether the number of 'a' characters is even, using the modulus operator %. String indexing is also employed to access and compare specific characters near the end of the string.



Source Code : 

#include <iostream>

#include <string>

using namespace std;



bool endsWithAB(const string& input) {

    int len = input.length();

    if (len < 2) return false;

    return input[len - 2] == 'a' && input[len - 1] == 'b';

}





bool hasEvenNumberOfAs(const string& input) {

    int count = 0;

    for (char ch : input) {

        if (ch == 'a') count++;

    }

    return count % 2 == 0;

}



int main() {

    string input;

    cout << "Enter a string: ";

    cin >> input;



    bool condition1 = endsWithAB(input);

    bool condition2 = hasEvenNumberOfAs(input);



    if (condition1 && condition2) {

        cout << "The string ends with 'ab' AND has an even number of 'a's." << endl;

    } else {

        cout << "The string does not meet the required conditions." << endl;

        if (!condition1) cout << "It does not end with 'ab'" << endl;

        if (!condition2) cout << "It does not have an even number of 'a's" << endl;

    }



    return 0;

}



