This C++ program implements a recursive descent parser for a given context-free grammar. It takes a string input and checks whether it belongs to the language defined by the grammar:

S → b | AB, A → a | aA, B → b.

The program uses three recursive functions (S(), A(), and B()) to match the input against the grammar rules. If the entire input matches and is fully consumed, it prints Accepted; otherwise, it prints Rejected. This approach simulates how compilers parse syntax in source code. 

